WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:00.923
<v Instructor>So let's have a look</v>

2
00:00:00.923 --> 00:00:03.810
at some solution architectures for Amazon FSx.

3
00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:04.980
First of all, how do you migrate

4
00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:09.360
from a single AZ FSx for Windows file server to a multi-AZ?

5
00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.840
There is no option to just upgrade it and with multi-AZ yet

6
00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:15.990
so therefore you must do some kind of steps.

7
00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:18.780
So let's create first a multi-AZ version

8
00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:21.000
of FSx for Windows file server

9
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.850
and then we're going to use DataSync to send data

10
00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:27.960
from the single AZ to the multi-AZ file server.

11
00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:30.900
The great thing about this solution is that we remain

12
00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:33.180
up and the data sync happens in the background

13
00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:34.080
and then when we're ready

14
00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:36.360
we're going to migrate over to multi-AZ.

15
00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:39.120
So it's slower, but we maintain availability.

16
00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:40.530
The other option to go

17
00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:43.590
from a single AZ to multi-AZ is to do a backup

18
00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:46.500
of the first Windows file server, shut it down,

19
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.330
and then do a restore into a multi-AZ.

20
00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:50.340
So we're going to get some down time

21
00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:53.033
but at least we're going to be a bit quicker.

22
00:00:53.033 --> 00:00:56.610
So how would you go to decrease an FSx volume size?

23
00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:58.410
Well, if you take a backup, you should know

24
00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:01.650
that you can only restore to a same size volume

25
00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:04.890
and you can only increase capacity for a file system.

26
00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:06.540
You can never decrease it.

27
00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:10.440
So the way to do it is actually to do a create

28
00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:13.380
a FSx that is smaller and then you're going to

29
00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:16.500
use DataSync to sync data and migrate your app over.

30
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.600
So say you have a two terabytes FSx

31
00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:21.720
for Windows file server app file system,

32
00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:24.090
and it's going to be used by an application

33
00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:26.640
and you know you only use it say 500 gigabytes

34
00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.620
and you want to migrate to one terabyte.

35
00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:31.470
Therefore, you will create that one terabyte FSx

36
00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:34.230
for Windows file server and then use DataSync to

37
00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:36.660
migrate the data over continuously.

38
00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.430
And then when you have reached parity

39
00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:41.760
you can just migrate your application to use the new FSx

40
00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:45.243
for Windows file server and stop using the old one.

41
00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:47.640
Another feature you should know

42
00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.990
for FSx for Lustre is called data lazy loading.

43
00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:54.810
So any processing job on Lustre with S3

44
00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:57.090
as an input data source can be started

45
00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:00.990
without Lustre doing a full download of the dataset first.

46
00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:01.823
So what as that means?

47
00:02:01.823 --> 00:02:04.560
That means that if you have a huge S3 bucket, we're talking

48
00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.760
you know, petabytes and using Amazon FSx for Lustre

49
00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:11.070
then you can start using your application

50
00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:12.750
for data processing right away

51
00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:15.150
because the data is going to be lazy loaded.

52
00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:18.360
So that means that's only when a client is going to request

53
00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:21.926
for that specific data set, will FSx for Lustre download it

54
00:02:21.926 --> 00:02:24.660
and therefore is going to decrease your cost

55
00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:26.220
and decrease your latency.

56
00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:29.400
So your FSx for Lustre right now, the clients ask

57
00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:32.100
for a very specific dataset, so it's going to be loaded

58
00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:35.400
into FSx for Lustre and then processed by the clients.

59
00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.680
But the idea is that as soon as you have an S3 bucket

60
00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:39.210
you can just launch Amazon FSx

61
00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:42.270
for Lustre on top of it and start using it.

62
00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:43.556
And then of course the data is

63
00:02:43.556 --> 00:02:45.660
is going to be loaded only once.

64
00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:47.040
So if you already have a dataset

65
00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:49.950
in your Amazon FSx for Lustre file system

66
00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:52.620
then it's not going to be loaded a second time

67
00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:55.980
if it hasn't changed or if it's being requested by clients.

68
00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:59.040
So data lazy loading allows you to get the right

69
00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:01.680
data exactly when your client requested

70
00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:03.390
and that's a great feature.

71
00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:04.380
So that's it for this lecture

72
00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:07.330
I hope you liked it and I will see you in the next lecture.

